WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
Which of these neurons just fired?

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:04.600
Their shapes give little away.

3
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.500
But watch what happens when the cells contain a protein that changes how brightly it

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.960
glows. Now there is a change we can follow.

5
00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:18.353
Start with that cell.

6
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:24.433
An electrical pulse called a spike opens channels, which are gates that let calcium

7
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:30.873
enter. Calcium inside the cell body stays raised for a while after the pulse has

8
00:00:30.873 --> 00:00:31.873
passed.

9
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:43.233
The protein that makes this rise visible is a calcium indicator called GCaMP.

10
00:00:44.453 --> 00:00:47.753
When calcium binds, part of the protein closes a gap.

11
00:00:48.913 --> 00:00:53.293
That keeps water away from the part that gives off light, so the protein shines

12
00:00:53.293 --> 00:00:54.293
brighter.

13
00:00:54.817 --> 00:01:00.417
Calcium imaging follows electrical activity by measuring the light from changes in

14
00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:04.557
calcium. First measure the brightness before the response.

15
00:01:06.037 --> 00:01:10.137
Subtract that starting brightness from the current brightness, then divide the

16
00:01:10.137 --> 00:01:11.697
difference by the starting brightness.

17
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.020
The trace now shows how much the light changes relative to where it started.

18
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.080
As the cell brightens, the line rises above zero.

19
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.020
As the glow fades, the line comes back down.

20
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.910
A microscope can keep the glow from each cell separate, so you can see where

21
00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:41.870
activity changes. An optical fibre can collect light from a whole group of cells

22
00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:46.070
together. That shared readout is called fibre photometry.

23
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.720
Now return to one cell and watch it fire again while the calcium signal is still

24
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.780
fading. Will the light show two separate flashes, or will the responses run together

25
00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:04.780
in one swell?

26
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.060
The responses can run together.

27
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:17.740
The indicator brightens and fades slowly compared with the electrical pulse.

28
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.880
The next spike can add to the signal that's still fading.

29
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.980
A single swell can therefore contain several spikes.

30
00:02:27.083 --> 00:02:32.063
To check which spikes the glow can pick up, record the cell's electrical activity

31
00:02:32.063 --> 00:02:33.323
while you film it.

32
00:02:34.463 --> 00:02:36.043
Line up both records in time.

33
00:02:37.263 --> 00:02:41.683
That comparison tells you what the light can resolve under the conditions you

34
00:02:41.683 --> 00:02:42.683
tested.

35
00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:53.187
Across many cells, brightness patterns can show which cells respond alike.

36
00:02:54.567 --> 00:02:58.747
In 2016, Baden and colleagues imaged isolated mouse retinas.

37
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:03.700
They studied the retina's output neurons, called ganglion cells.

38
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.920
Grouping cells by how they respond to light defines functional types.

39
00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.140
They distinguished at least 32 types.

40
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.710
You can also ask what makes each cell respond.

41
00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:22.750
In 2015, Barretto and colleagues imaged a cluster of taste neurons, the geniculate

42
00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.830
ganglion, in anaesthetized mice.

43
00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.010
Taste solutions flowed over the tongue.

44
00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:34.430
Most responding neurons were strongly activated by only one of the tested taste

45
00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:34.910
qualities.
